# 2D Slices in Go

Two-dimensional (2D) slices in Go are **slices of slices** — each element of the outer slice is itself a slice. This is very useful for representing grids, matrices, game boards, and layered data structures.

## Creating a 2D Matrix

```go
// 3x4 matrix with make
rows := 3
cols := 4
matrix := make([][]int, rows)
for i := range matrix {
    matrix[i] = make([]int, cols)
}

// Fill values
for i := 0; i < rows; i++ {
    for j := 0; j < cols; j++ {
        matrix[i][j] = i*cols + j + 1
    }
}
```

## Matrix Literal

```go
grid := [][]int{
    {1, 0, 0},
    {0, 1, 0},
    {0, 0, 1},
}
fmt.Println(grid[1][1]) // 1
```

## Dynamic Sizing (Jagged Arrays)

Unlike other languages, rows in a Go 2D slice can have **different lengths**:

```go
jagged := [][]int{
    {1},
    {2, 3},
    {4, 5, 6},
}
for _, row := range jagged {
    fmt.Println(row)
}
```

## Example: Tic-Tac-Toe Board

```go
const (Empty = "."; X = "X"; O = "O")
type Board [3][3]string

func checkWinner(b Board) string {
    for _, row := range b {
        if row[0] != Empty && row[0] == row[1] && row[1] == row[2] {
            return row[0]
        }
    }
    // Check columns and diagonals...
    return ""
}
```

## Memory-Efficient Allocation

```go
// Single allocation for better cache performance
buf := make([]int, rows*cols)
matrix := make([][]int, rows)
for i := range matrix {
    matrix[i] = buf[i*cols : (i+1)*cols]
}
```

